NAME
    edit — Open a file in the built-in terminal text editor.

SYNOPSIS
    edit <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'edit' command opens a file using the built-in terminal text editor.
    It allows you to modify the contents of a file directly from the CLI.

    No graphical editor required. Just you, your keyboard, and a questionable
    number of characters waiting to be typed.

ARGUMENTS
    file    The file to open in the terminal editor.
    Can be a relative or absolute path.

USAGE
    > edit file.txt
        Opens file.txt in the terminal editor.

    > edit notes.txt
        Opens notes.txt so it can be modified.

EXAMPLES
    > edit README.txt
        Opens README.txt for editing.

    > edit config.ini
        Opens config.ini in the built-in editor.

FUN FACTS
    - The editor runs entirely inside the terminal.
    - You do not need nano, vim, or another external editor.
    - Files can be edited without leaving the CLI.
    - If you make a mistake, remember: Ctrl+Z is your friend.

